WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.260
Universal's "The Last Voyage of the&nbsp;
Demeter" bombed at the box office after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:09.000
it opened on August 11. So why did the&nbsp;
"Dracula on a boat" flick flop so hard?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.320
"Demeter" netted $6.5 million on its&nbsp;
first weekend in theaters, making back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.760
just a fraction of its $45 million budget.&nbsp;
Perhaps the most immediate culprit behind its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.800
lackluster financial performance is its lukewarm&nbsp;
reception from critics. On Rotten Tomatoes, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.620
film sits at 51% from professional reviewers and&nbsp;
a still unimpressive 74% from audiences at large,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.900
as of the making of this video. In his review&nbsp;
for Variety, for example, Owen Gleiberman wrote,

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.360
"'The Last Voyage of the Demeter' is too explicit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.540
too dawdling yet rapid-fire, too&nbsp;
much like other horror films."

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:44.340
Critics weren't wholly unkind, however —&nbsp;
RogerEbert.com reviewer Peter Sobczynski&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.220
awarded it three-and-a-half stars&nbsp;
out of a possible four, writing,

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.940
"It is a smart, well-made, and sometimes&nbsp;
downright creepy take on the tale that both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.180
horror buffs and regular moviegoers&nbsp;
can appreciate in equal measure."

00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:56.775
"We call him Dracula."

00:00:56.775 --> 00:01:00.000
But bad reviews alone aren't typically&nbsp;
enough to keep viewers away from an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.540
otherwise interesting release, suggesting&nbsp;
that at least a couple other key factors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.320
beyond just its critical reception played a&nbsp;
part in the film's subpar box office haul.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:12.540
Before taking into account how reviewers received&nbsp;
"Demeter," a multitude of factors regarding its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.100
promotion and release made its success an uphill&nbsp;
battle prior, even, to its theatrical premiere.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.400
Chief among what might be considered&nbsp;
marketing failures is the fact that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.300
film's title doesn't accurately reflect its&nbsp;
biggest selling point. "The Last Voyage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.740
the Demeter" is technically a classic Dracula&nbsp;
movie, adapting a chapter from Bram Stoker's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.180
genre-defining vampire novel. Its title,&nbsp;
however, simply evokes a journey at sea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.120
leaving casual viewers unaware&nbsp;
that it's actually a Dracula story.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.320
Several international markets feature distinctive&nbsp;
names for the film that take advantage of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.240
name-recognition that Dracula has. For example, in&nbsp;
Australia, the film is titled "Dracula: Voyage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.333
the Demeter," a far more inviting name that paints&nbsp;
a clearer picture as to what audiences can expect.

00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:54.720
Universal also decided to release a horror movie&nbsp;
in August, a couple months before spooky movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.860
season officially kicks off near the start of&nbsp;
October. Not only is this timing not ideal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.860
but it falls squarely in the midst&nbsp;
of "Barbenheimer" mania, during which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.180
theater-goers are still more likely to see either&nbsp;
of those two box office giants than an ill-timed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.320
horror movie that bewilderingly buries the fact&nbsp;
that it's about fiction's most iconic vampire.

00:02:10.403 --> 00:02:11.303
"So cool."

00:02:11.303 --> 00:02:14.160
While it's difficult to deny the&nbsp;
competition "Demeter" faced from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.700
both "Barbie" and "Oppenheimer," it should&nbsp;
be noted that the Universal Pictures flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.240
wasn't the only horror film competing for the&nbsp;
attention of audience members at the multiplex.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:25.500
During the Dracula film's opening weekend,&nbsp;
audiences had multiple options to choose from.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.860
During its debut weekend, "Demeter"&nbsp;
faced competition from both Disney's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:34.680
"Haunted Mansion" and A24's "Talk to Me."&nbsp;
They made $5.7 million and $5.1 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.820
respectively, almost in line with the&nbsp;
Dracula film's $6.5 million debut.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.960
When it came to that weekend's horror&nbsp;
offerings, the trio definitely cannibalized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.020
one another. While "Demeter" technically&nbsp;
took the higher spot of the three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.700
it's clear the crowded horror landscape robbed&nbsp;
the Dracula film of a potential $10 million debut.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.520
"Haunted Mansion" appeals to&nbsp;
both families and teenagers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.840
thanks in part to its PG-13 rating.&nbsp;
Seeing as "Demeter" boasts an R-rating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.440
a portion of prospective viewers likely&nbsp;
went with Disney's haunted offering. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.100
R-rated "Talk to Me," meanwhile, is thriving,&nbsp;
thanks to positive word of mouth and reviews.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.260
"Demeter" was the new film in cinemas, but it was&nbsp;
arguably the least interesting, at least from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:15.960
audience's perspective. Paying cinemagoers gave&nbsp;
the Dracula film a B- CinemaScore, signaling mixed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.460
to negative word of mouth. "Haunted Mansion" and&nbsp;
"Talk to Me," meanwhile, boast B+ CinemaScores.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.836 --> 00:03:25.096
Perhaps "Demeter" would have shined earlier in&nbsp;
the summer, when horror films were more scarce.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:29.700
Even if international audiences were interested&nbsp;
in seeing "The Last Voyage of the Demeter," key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:33.600
markets weren't given the opportunity to do&nbsp;
so. The film's release in the United Kingdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.080
has been delayed indefinitely thanks&nbsp;
to eOne shutting down operations in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.683
the country. With eOne now sold to Lionsgate,&nbsp;
"Demeter" has lost a distributor in the UK.

00:03:41.683 --> 00:03:44.323
"May God have mercy on us all!"

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:48.180
Only time will tell if the Universal project&nbsp;
will receive a theatrical release in the UK.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.920
Seeing as how "Demeter" features a largely Irish&nbsp;
cast and with a majority of the story dependent on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:57.840
UK locations, it must be difficult for Universal&nbsp;
Pictures to bid farewell to such a key territory.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.560
Other international markets haven't&nbsp;
received the film as of yet. /Film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.160
reports that the flick is slated to debut&nbsp;
in Spain and India, though no official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.000
release dates have been given. With the film&nbsp;
bombing stateside, it remains to be seen if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.960
Universal Pictures will move forward with&nbsp;
international releases or cut their losses.

00:04:13.656 --> 00:04:17.400
A few months before "Demeter" premiered&nbsp;
in theaters, Universal's previous vampire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:21.180
movie "Renfield" also bombed. This&nbsp;
was sort of a canary in the coal mine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.760
foretelling its successor's&nbsp;
lack of box office success.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.440
"Renfield" stars Nicholas Hoult as R. M.&nbsp;
Renfield, a character straight out of Bram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.000
Stoker's "Dracula" novel. Similarities to its&nbsp;
literary source material pretty much start and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.480
stop there, however, given that "Renfield"&nbsp;
is a quirky comedy set in the present day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.619 --> 00:04:41.059
Audiences were ultimately uninterested in&nbsp;
this twist on the classic Dracula formula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.059 --> 00:04:43.800
leaving its total box office haul&nbsp;
short of even half its budget.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.120
That "Demeter" is on track to fare worse is&nbsp;
hardly surprising, then, given how "Renfield"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.020
demonstrated that its overarching formula&nbsp;
isn't something most theater-goers want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.400
see. And whereas "Renfield" stars big names&nbsp;
like Hoult, Awkwafina, and Nicholas Cage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.780
"Demeter" is absent a marquee star to make up&nbsp;
for its core concept failing to draw viewers.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:05.880
This phenomenon, in fact, extends past just&nbsp;
vampire movies. Both Guy Ritchie's "King Arthur:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.780
Legend of the Sword" and the 2018 "Robin&nbsp;
Hood" film — while not quite on the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:13.500
level — failed to make back their budgets as&nbsp;
well, further indicating that audiences don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:17.520
care about contemporary spins on classic&nbsp;
material as much as studios seem to think.

